---
// The flagship post. Direct answer first, real structure, GEO-quotable blocks.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { client } from '../../data/client.js';

const tel = client.phone.replace(/\s/g, '');
const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'How much does a rewire cost in Leeds? 2026 prices',
  datePublished: '2026-07-28',
  dateModified: '2026-07-29',
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: client.owner, jobTitle: 'Electrician' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://eselec.co.uk/#business' },
};
const breadcrumbs = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://eselec.co.uk/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://eselec.co.uk/blog/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Rewire cost in Leeds', item: 'https://eselec.co.uk/blog/rewire-cost-leeds/' },
  ],
};

// The ONE shared rewire model: same numbers as the home-page Reckoner
// and the services table (client.reckoner).
const HOUSE = { 2: '2-bed terrace', 3: '3-bed semi', 4: '4-bed detached', 5: '5-bed detached' };
const table = Object.entries(client.reckoner.beds).map(([n, m]) => [
  HOUSE[n],
  `£${m.low.toLocaleString('en-GB')} to £${m.high.toLocaleString('en-GB')}`,
  `${m.daysLow} to ${m.daysHigh} days`,
]);
---
<Base
  title="How Much Does a Rewire Cost in Leeds? 2026 Prices"
  description="A full rewire in Leeds costs £3,200 to £8,500 in 2026 depending on size. A Leeds electrician breaks down real prices per house type and what moves them."
  current="/blog/"
  schema={[article, breadcrumbs]}
>
  <article class="paper">
    <div class="wrap article">
      <header class="article__head">
        <h1>How much does a rewire cost in Leeds? 2026 prices</h1>
        <p class="article__meta">
          By {client.owner}, electrician, ES Elec · July 2026 <!-- TEMP author -->
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="article__body">
        <p>
          A full house rewire in Leeds costs between £3,200 and £8,500 in 2026.
          A 2-bed terrace sits at the bottom of that range, a 5-bed detached at
          the top. Partial rewires run £1,200 to £2,800. Those are working
          figures from jobs quoted this year, not national averages: Leeds
          labour rates run under London by a good margin, and most of the
          housing stock here is terraces and semis that wire predictably.
          <!-- TEMP figures throughout -->
        </p>

        <table class="costs">
          <caption>
            Full rewire, materials and labour, certificate and notification
            included. Your written quote is the real number; this table is for
            orientation.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">House</th><th scope="col">Days</th><th scope="col">2026 price</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {table.map(([house, price, days]) => (
              <tr><td><strong>{house}</strong></td><td>{days}</td><td>{price}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          Living in the house through the rewire? Add about
          £{client.reckoner.occupied.price} and a couple of days across the
          board. That covers sheeting, furniture moves and keeping your power
          on every night, and it's the same modifier the Reckoner on my
          <a href="/">home page</a> uses. <!-- TEMP model -->
        </p>

        <h2>What moves the price</h2>
        <p>
          Walls and floors first: solid floors and lath-and-plaster walls slow
          the chasing right down. Then how much you want moved rather than
          replaced like for like. Then the finish: brushed steel sockets across
          a whole house add hundreds on their own. Occupied houses go slower
          than empty ones because putting your rooms back together every
          evening is real work. Nobody itemises that last one, which is why two
          quotes for the same house can sit £1,000 apart.
        </p>

        <h2>Do you need a full rewire at all?</h2>
        <p>
          Probably not, if the wiring is PVC and the problems are localised. A
          house fails on specifics, not on age. An EICR (£140, half a morning)
          tells you what state yours is in before you commit to anything. I
          have talked more than one caller out of a rewire after the inspection
          came back cleaner than they feared. Rubber or fabric-insulated cable,
          though, or a fuse box with a wooden back: stop reading and book the
          inspection this week.
        </p>

        <h2>How to read a rewire quote</h2>
        <p>
          Four things to demand before you compare numbers. An itemised
          room-by-room breakdown: if it's one number on a text, you're not
          comparing quotes, you're comparing guesses. Who makes good the
          plaster, in writing. Whether testing and certification are inside
          the price. And the payment schedule. The cheapest of three quotes
          is usually the one that's missing a line.
        </p>

        <h2>What happens on the job</h2>
        <p>
          Day one is protection and first fix upstairs. Power stays on
          overnight, every night. Kitchens and bathrooms get planned around
          your mornings. Last day is testing, every circuit, and you get the
          EIC certificate before the van leaves the street.
        </p>

        <div class="band">
          <h2 style="font-size: 1.4rem;">Want the real number for your house?</h2>
          <p style="color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--t-small);">
            Room-by-room written price, usually within 48 hours of a look.
          </p>
          <div class="acts">
            <a class="btn btn--call" href={`tel:${tel}`}>Call Eddie</a>
            <a class="btn btn--quote" href="/#quote">Get a quote</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
